Formatter-closure regression (2026-07-25 amendment): a case body's prose that
happens to contain the bare word "case" must never be reflowed so that word
starts a line — the parser would then read it as a real arm label. At the fmt
corpus/property-test width (80 cols), 36 "x" words is one of the exact lengths
where gsx fmt's word-gap fill must wrap right at that boundary (probed by
sweeping word counts 1..100 before picking this one, so the golden actually
exercises the bond rather than merely fitting on one line). The printer's
case-body bond keeps "case 1:" off line start, so re-parsing the formatted
output still yields exactly one selected arm and the same render. The property
is checked by internal/printer's TestCorpusInputsProperty, which formats every
semantic corpus input.gsx and asserts the re-parsed AST is unchanged.
-- input.gsx --
package views

component Prose(k string) {
	<div>{ switch k {
	case "a":
		x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x case 1: nope
	default:
		<i>y</i>
	} }</div>
}
-- invoke --
Prose("a")
-- diagnostics.golden --
-- render.golden --
<div>x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x x case 1: nope</div>
-- generated.x.go.golden --
